  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery shows mainly clear skies across\neastern Idaho, though localized pockets of low stratus have \ndeveloped up around Island Park and in the far southeastern \ncorner near Bear Lake. The region resides between departing and \nincoming upper-level troughs today, allowing dry conditions to \nreturn under partly cloudy skies once morning stratus erodes. \nIncreasing sunshine and weak ridging will enable afternoon \ntemperatures to rebound by roughly 5 degrees compared to \nSaturday's readings. The next Pacific low-pressure system will \nbegin slowly pushing inland tonight and Monday. Model guidance \ncontinues to trend slower with the eastward progression of this \ntrough, keeping the first half of Monday dry for most locations \nand confining incoming afternoon moisture primarily to the \nCentral Mountains. Cloud cover and afternoon precipitation will \nknock daytime temperatures down a couple of degrees across the \nCentral Mountains and south-central Idaho on Monday, while areas\nfarther east experience little to no change from Sunday's \nhighs.\n\nThe storm system finally swings across Idaho Monday night \nthrough Tuesday night, though guidance continues to show a \npronounced trend toward splitting\u2014similar to Saturday's system. \nThis split-flow evolution introduces notable uncertainty and \nkeeps forecast confidence low regarding specific, definitive \nrain and snow totals. Current National Blend of Models (NBM) QPF\nguidance depicts general liquid totals up to 0.20 inches across\nthe Snake River Plain, Magic Valley, and low-to-mid elevation \nmountain valleys. In favored upslope areas\u2014including the Wood \nRiver Valley, surrounding Central Mountains, and along the \nMontana border\u2014totals are pushing 0.50 inches, with a similar \npattern extending from the South Hills and Albion Mountains \neastward through the Bear River Range. Higher elevations across \nthe remaining mountain ranges are projected to receive 0.25 to \n0.35 inches.\n\nAhead of the main frontal passage, snow levels will hold \nbetween 6,500 and 8,000 feet before colder air overspreads all \nof eastern Idaho on Tuesday. Tuesday's lower-elevation snow \npotential will come down to a classic race between cold air \nadvection arriving and precipitation tapering off on the back \nside of the departing low. During this frontal transition, snow \nlevels are forecast to drop to between 5,000 and 6,500 feet. The\nlower bound of model guidance brings snow levels low enough for\nsnowflakes to fall on valley floors, though surface \ntemperatures will make accumulation difficult. That all being \nsaid, there remains considerable uncertainty with this part of \nthe forecast. GFS and its ensembles are noticeably snowier at \nlower elevations compared to those with the ECMWF. Overall, \nhigher confidence exists with snow down to around 6,000 feet, \nwhile impactful accumulations remain confined above 7,000 feet \nbut things will remain in flux for a few more forecasts in all \nlikelihood. \n\nThe mid-to-late portion of next week will remain cool and\nunsettled as broad upper-level troughing anchors over the\nPacific Northwest and ejects periodic waves of embedded moisture\nacross the Intermountain West. Persistent cold air will restrict\ndaytime valley highs to the upper 30s and lower 40s from Tuesday\nstraight into next weekend, accompanied by daily chances for\nlight snow. While substantial ensemble spread remains regarding\nhow individual waves evolve late next week, the colder thermal\nprofile means that if organized moisture overlaps with these\ntemperatures, accumulating snow down to valley floors is\ncertainly possible.",
